{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"jaded-pugnacious-isopod","creation_channel":"cli","description":"## The hazard\n\n`terraform plan` run from a checkout that is behind `origin/main` reports **\"No changes\"** for\nresources that are declared on `main` but absent from the local tree. That output is\nindistinguishable from the healthy case, so an operator or agent reads \"infrastructure matches\ndeclaration\" when the truth is \"this tree does not contain the declaration\".\n\n## Observed, 2026-09-05\n\nThe main checkout at `/Users/joeoakhart/rebar` was **14 commits behind `origin/main`**.\n`monitoring_autodeploy.tf` there was 355 lines with 6 alarms; on `origin/main` it is 624 lines with\n11. Running `terraform plan` from that checkout returned 91 `resource_changes`, **all `no-op`** —\n`Plan: 0 to add, 0 to change, 0 to destroy`.\n\nFive CloudWatch alarms from epic 6202 stories S2 and S3 were declared on `main` and **did not exist\nin AWS**. Confirmed independently with `aws cloudwatch describe-alarms` before the apply. An apply\nfrom the stale tree would have succeeded, changed nothing, and been reported as done.\n\nRunning the same plan from a tree at `origin/main`, against the same S3 remote state, returned\n`Plan: 5 to add, 1 to change, 0 to destroy`.\n\n## The worse direction\n\nUnder-application is the benign failure. The dangerous one is the reverse: if a future commit\n**removes** a resource, an apply from a tree that predates that commit proposes **recreating** it —\nso a stale checkout does not merely fail to converge, it can actively undo intended deletions.\nNeither direction produces a warning.\n\n## Why this belongs with tonight's other findings\n\nIt is the same class that has recurred repeatedly in this repo: **an operation that did not happen,\nreported as success.** The Terraform Drift workflow was blinded for two days by a whitespace defect\nbefore `plan` ever ran (`chiselled-glandulous-fantail`); a CI job cast a passing vote without\nexecuting; a probe silenced itself and its absence read as health. Here the plan genuinely ran and\ngenuinely found nothing — because it was asked the wrong question.\n\nNote the drift workflow has the same exposure: `terraform-drift.yml` fails on a non-empty plan, so\nit is only meaningful if it runs against current `main`. If it ever ran from a stale ref it would\nreport clean for exactly this reason.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] AC1 - A Terraform plan or apply executed against a tree that is behind the tracked branch is\n      DETECTED and reported as such, distinctly from \"no changes needed\". A reader must be able to\n      tell \"your tree is stale\" from \"infrastructure matches\".\n- [ ] AC2 - The check is not defeated by the stale tree itself — i.e. it does not rely solely on a\n      file that the stale tree also lacks. State how it establishes currency.\n- [ ] AC3 - The daily drift workflow provably runs against current `main`, and that is asserted\n      rather than assumed.\n- [ ] AC4 - Defect-seeded: reproduce the observed case (a tree behind `main` whose plan is empty\n      while `main`'s is not) and show the check fires. A check that would not have caught the\n      2026-09-05 instance has not been shown to work.\n- [ ] AC5 - Portable per `project.portability`: works for a developer running terraform locally with\n      no CI provider, not only inside a workflow.\n\n## Non-goals\n\nNot preventing local terraform runs from a feature branch — that is legitimate. The requirement is\nthat the staleness is VISIBLE, not that it is forbidden. Not fixing the fmt-before-plan ordering,\nwhich is `chiselled-glandulous-fantail`.\n\n## Note on the local checkout specifically\n\nThis ticket is about the missing SIGNAL, not about one checkout. The checkout can be fast-forwarded\nby its operator at any time; it was deliberately NOT fast-forwarded during this incident because\nAGENTS.md forbids fast-forwarding the shared main checkout (it may hold uncommitted operator WIP)\nand because other agents were actively working against it.","id":"eebc-6aa1-e45b-4325","parent_id":"","priority":2,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"bug","title":"terraform plan from a checkout behind origin/main reports 'No changes' for resources declared on main, indistinguishable from a converged infrastructure — and the reverse case would propose recreating deleted resources"},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1788569581843605001,"uuid":"928bb751-4e66-486b-87cd-1d050d35fdf0"}